{"name":"Free Speech Aquamarine","hex":"#029d74","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"free","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Unconstrained.","Not imprisoned or enslaved."]},{"part":"Adverb","definitions":["Without needing to pay.","Freely; willingly."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/free"},{"term":"speech","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The ability to speak; the faculty of uttering words or articulate sounds and vocalizations to communicate.","The act of speaking, a certain style of it."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To make (a speech); to harangue."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/speech"},{"term":"aquamarine","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The bluish-green colour of the sea.","A transparent bluish-green, sometimes yellow-green, variety of beryl."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a bluish-green colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/aquamarine"}],"entries":[{"title":"Freedom of speech","blocks":[{"text":"Freedom of speech is a principle that supports the freedom of an individual or a community to articulate their opinions and ideas without fear of retaliation, censorship, or legal sanction. The right to freedom of expression has been recognised as a human right in the Universal Declaration of Human Rights (UDHR) and international human rights law. Many countries have constitutional laws that protect freedom of speech. Terms such as free speech, freedom of speech, and freedom of expression are often used interchangeably in political discourse. However, in legal contexts, freedom of expression more broadly encompasses the right to seek, receive, and impart information or ideas, regardless of the medium used."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Eleanor_Roosevelt_UDHR_%2827758131387%29.jpg/330px-Eleanor_Roosevelt_UDHR_%2827758131387%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Freedom_of_speech","wikidata":"Q22692"},{"title":"Speech","blocks":[{"text":"Speech is the use of the human voice as a medium for language. Spoken language combines vowel and consonant sounds to form units of meaning like words, which belong to a language's lexicon. There are many different intentional speech acts, such as informing, declaring, asking, persuading, directing; acts may vary in various aspects like enunciation, intonation, loudness, and tempo to convey meaning. Individuals may also unintentionally communicate aspects of their social position through speech, such as sex, age, place of origin, physiological and mental condition, education, and experiences."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/Real-time_MRI_-_Speaking_%28English%29.ogv/250px--Real-time_MRI_-_Speaking_%28English%29.ogv.jpg","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Speech","wikidata":"Q52946"},{"title":"Aquamarine (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Aquamarine is a pale blue-green color between cyan and cerulean on the color wheel. It is named after the mineral aquamarine, a gemstone mainly found in granite rocks. The first recorded use of aquamarine as a color name in English was in 1598."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2a/Aquamarine.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aquamarine_(color)","wikidata":"Q372973"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Orator_at_Speakers_Corner%2C_London%2C_with_crowd%2C_1974.jpg/500px-Orator_at_Speakers_Corner%2C_London%2C_with_crowd%2C_1974.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Orator_at_Speakers_Corner%2C_London%2C_with_crowd%2C_1974.jpg/500px-Orator_at_Speakers_Corner%2C_London%2C_with_crowd%2C_1974.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Orator at London's Speakers' Corner, 1974","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOrator_at_Speakers_Corner%2C_London%2C_with_crowd%2C_1974.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/Index_Librorum_Prohibitorum_1.jpg/500px-Index_Librorum_Prohibitorum_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/Index_Librorum_Prohibitorum_1.jpg/500px-Index_Librorum_Prohibitorum_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Title page of Index Librorum Prohibitorum, or List of Prohibited Books (Venice, 1564)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AIndex_Librorum_Prohibitorum_1.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/db/Henric_van_Cuyck_Panegyricae_orationes_septem_1596.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/db/Henric_van_Cuyck_Panegyricae_orationes_septem_1596.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"In Panegyricae orationes septem (1596), Henric van Cuyck, a Dutch Bishop, defended the need for censorship and argued that Johannes Gutenberg's printing press had resulted in a world infected by \"pernicious lies\"—so van Cuyck singled out the Talmud and the Qur'an, and the writings of Martin Luther, Jean Calvin and Erasmus of Rotterdam.[22]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHenric_van_Cuyck_Panegyricae_orationes_septem_1596.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Areopagitica_bridwell.jpg/330px-Areopagitica_bridwell.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Areopagitica_bridwell.jpg/330px-Areopagitica_bridwell.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"First page of John Milton's 1644 edition of Areopagitica, in which he argued forcefully against the Licensing Order of 1643","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAreopagitica_bridwell.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Jacobus_de_Voragine_Legenda_aurea_sanctorum.jpg/330px-Jacobus_de_Voragine_Legenda_aurea_sanctorum.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Jacobus_de_Voragine_Legenda_aurea_sanctorum.jpg/330px-Jacobus_de_Voragine_Legenda_aurea_sanctorum.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"This 1688 edition of Jacobus de Voragine's Golden Legend (1260) was censored according to the Index Librorum Expurgatorum of 1707, which listed the specific passages of books already in circulation that required censorship.[26][27]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AJacobus_de_Voragine_Legenda_aurea_sanctorum.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/George_Orwell_statue_-_BBC_London_%2838562767202%29.jpg/500px-George_Orwell_statue_-_BBC_London_%2838562767202%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/George_Orwell_statue_-_BBC_London_%2838562767202%29.jpg/1280px-George_Orwell_statue_-_BBC_London_%2838562767202%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/George_Orwell_statue_-_BBC_London_%2838562767202%29.jpg/500px-George_Orwell_statue_-_BBC_London_%2838562767202%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"George Orwell statue at the headquarters of the BBC. A defence of free speech in an open society, the wall behind the statue is inscribed with the words \"If liberty means anythi